course: books
lesson: 75
title: Just So Stories (Rudyard Kipling), 2: How the Camel got his Hump
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When the world was brand new and the animals were just starting to work for Man, the Camel lived alone in the Howling Desert, munching thorns, and to everyone who spoke to him he said only "Humph!"
explain: The Horse, the Dog and the Ox each ask him to come and work like them, and each gets a "Humph!", so the Man makes the Three work double to cover for the lazy Camel, and they are furious.
explain: The Djinn (a magic spirit) in charge of All Deserts finds the Camel admiring himself in a pool and warns him not to say "Humph!" again; the Camel says it anyway, and his back puffs up into a great lolloping "humph".
explain: The Djinn explains that the hump lets him work three days without eating, to make up for the three days he missed; the Camel joins the Three but has never caught up, and has never learned to behave.
explain: The poem after the story turns it into a lesson for us: if we have too little to do we "get the hump" and go grumpy, and the cure is to get outside and dig.
fact: The Camel lived in "a Howling Desert because he did not want to work", eating "sticks and thorns and tamarisks and milkweed and prickles"; "'scruciating idle" is short for "excruciatingly idle", terribly lazy.
fact: On Monday morning the Horse came first, "with a saddle on his back and a bit in his mouth" and asked him to trot; the Dog came "with a stick in his mouth" to fetch and carry; the Ox came "with the yoke on his neck" to plough.
fact: Each time the Camel said "Humph!" and the animal "went away and told the Man".
fact: The Man said the Three "must work double-time to make up for it", which made them "very angry (with the world so new-and-all)".
fact: They held "a palaver, and an indaba, and a punchayet, and a pow-wow": four words from different lands that all mean a meeting to talk things over. The Camel came by "chewing milkweed most 'scruciating idle" and laughed at them.
fact: A "Djinn" (genie) is a magic spirit from Arabian stories; this one travels "rolling in a cloud of dust", and calls the Camel "My long and bubbling friend" and "Bubbles", because camels grumble and bubble.
fact: The Djinn warned, "I shouldn't say that again if I were you; you might say it once too often."
fact: The Camel's back puffed up "into a great big lolloping humph"; the Camel had done no work from Monday to Thursday, so he had missed three days.
fact: The Djinn said, "You will be able to work now for three days without eating, because you can live on your humph."
fact: "We call it 'hump' now, not to hurt his feelings"; in British English "to get the hump" means to get grumpy and sulky.
fact: Real biology: a camel's hump is a store of fat, not water; when food is short the camel lives on that fat, and the hump goes floppy and small.
fact: Real biology: camels can go many days without drinking because their bodies waste very little water, and a thirsty camel can gulp around a hundred litres in a few minutes.
fact: Real biology: dromedary camels (Arabia, North Africa) have one hump and Bactrian camels (Central Asia) have two; people have kept camels to carry loads and give milk for thousands of years.
fact: Real biology: camels really do eat thorny desert plants; their tough lips and mouths are not hurt by the thorns.
fact: The poem says the cure for the hump "is not to sit still, / Or frowst with a book by the fire" ("frowst" = laze in a stuffy warm room), "But to take a large hoe and a shovel also, / And dig till you gently perspire" (sweat a little).
exercise: Where did the Camel live? | In the middle of a Howling Desert | On a raft in the middle of the sea | In a field next to the Man's house | By the white cliffs of Albion
exercise: Why did the Camel live there? | He did not want to work | He was hiding from the Djinn | The Man had sent him away | He liked the cool weather
exercise: What did the Camel say whenever anybody spoke to him? | "Humph!" | "Nice but nubbly!" | "Go away!" | "I'm hungry!"
exercise: What does "'scruciating idle" mean? | Terribly lazy | Very hungry | Quite angry | Far too tired
exercise: Who came to the Camel first, on Monday morning? | The Horse | The Dog | The Ox | The Djinn
exercise: What did the Horse have with him? | A saddle on his back and a bit in his mouth | A stick in his mouth to fetch and carry | A yoke on his neck for pulling the plough | A cloud of dust rolling all around him
exercise: What did the Dog want the Camel to do? | Fetch and carry | Trot fast | Plough | Dig
exercise: What work did the Ox do? | Ploughing | Trotting | Fetching sticks | Swimming
exercise: What did each animal do after the Camel said "Humph!"? | Went away and told the Man | Kicked the Camel very hard | Sat down and cried in the sand | Ran to fetch the Djinn at once
exercise: What did the Man decide to do about the Camel? | Leave him alone and make the Three work double-time | Go into the desert himself and drag him out by the neck | Give the Camel's work to the 'Stute Fish in the sea | Ask the Djinn to turn the Camel into a horse instead
exercise: What are a palaver, an indaba, a punchayet and a pow-wow? | All words for a meeting | Four kinds of desert plant | Names of the four animals | Four magic spells
exercise: What did the Camel do while the Three held their meeting? | Came by chewing milkweed and laughed at them | Hid behind a rock and listened to every word | Ran to the Man and said he was sorry at last | Fell asleep in the shade of a thorny bush
exercise: How does the Djinn travel? | Rolling in a cloud of dust | Riding on the Camel's back | Flying on a magic carpet | Swimming through the sand
exercise: What was the Camel doing when the Djinn found him? | Looking at his own reflection in a pool of water | Ploughing a field next to the angry Ox and Horse | Chewing sticks and thorns behind a sand dune | Sleeping on his back under a palm tree in the sun
exercise: What did the Djinn call the Camel? | "My long and bubbling friend" | "Noble and generous Cetacean" | "My lazy and humpless friend" | "Rash and silly young traveller"
exercise: What did the Djinn warn the Camel? | Not to say "Humph!" again, or he might say it once too often | Not to look in the pool again, or he might fall into it | Not to eat any more thorns, or he would get a tummy-ache | Not to go near the Horse, or the Horse would kick him
exercise: What happened when the Camel said "Humph!" once more? | His back puffed up into a great lolloping hump | His long neck shrank until it was very short | He fell into the pool of water with a splash | His legs grew longer and longer and longer
exercise: On which day did the Djinn give the Camel his hump? | Thursday | Monday | Sunday | Saturday
exercise: How many days of work had the Camel missed? | Three | Two | Four | Seven
exercise: How long can the Camel work without eating, says the Djinn? | Three days | One week | Two days | Ten whole years
exercise: Why do we now say "hump" and not "humph"? | Not to hurt the Camel's feelings | Because the Djinn told us to | Because "humph" is too hard to spell | Because the Horse said so
exercise: Has the Camel ever caught up with the days he missed? | No, never, and he has never learned to behave | Yes, after three days he was quite caught up | Yes, but only by working on every Sunday | No, because he ran back into the desert again
exercise: Real biology: what is inside a camel's hump? | Mostly fat | Mostly water | Mostly bone | Mostly air
exercise: Real biology: how many humps does a dromedary camel have? | One | Two | Three | None
exercise: According to the poem, what is the cure for "the hump"? | Taking a hoe and a shovel and digging till you sweat a little | Sitting very still and reading a good book by the fire | Staying in bed with the curtains closed till it goes away | Going to the zoo to look at the Camel and laugh at him
exercise: Thinking back: what did the Whale in the first story eat at the start? | Every fish in the sea but one | Only sticks, prickles and thorns | Cake and currants | Grass and milkweed
exercise: Thinking back: who asked the Whale "have you ever tasted Man?" | The small 'Stute Fish | The shipwrecked Mariner | The Djinn | The Dog
test: Ava says: "So the camel's hump is a big water tank, that's why he don't get thirsty." Put her right. | The hump stores fat; the body saves water in other ways | Yes, the hump is full of water from the desert pool | The hump is full of air to keep the camel cool | The hump is full of the thorns the camel eats
test: Gramps, Gen X: "That Camel was totally bogus, man, just chilling while the others worked." ("bogus" = bad, not fair.) Who had to do his work? | The Horse, the Dog and the Ox | The Man and the Djinn | The Whale and the 'Stute Fish | Nobody, the work was left
test: What would have happened if the Camel had listened to the Djinn's warning? | He might never have got his hump | He would have got two humps instead | The Djinn would have given him wings | He would have been made king of the desert
test: Rhys, Wales: "Our Mam says I've got the hump today, I'm proper tamping." ("tamping" = really angry.) What does "got the hump" mean? | Grumpy and sulky | Very tired and sleepy | A sore back from lifting | Hungry for some lunch
test: True or false: the Djinn gives the Camel the hump only to punish him. | False: it also lets him work three days without eating | True: the hump is no use to the Camel at all, ever | True: the Djinn wants the Camel to stay in the desert | False: the Camel asks the Djinn for it as a present
test: Kai, Gen Z: "Camel stared at his reflection all day? Delulu, he thinks he ate." ("delulu" = fooling himself, "ate" = looked amazing.) Where was the Camel looking when the Djinn came? | Into a pool, at his own reflection | Up at the Djinn's cloud of dust | Out across the desert for the Man | Down at the thorns
test: Why does the story keep saying "with the world so new-and-all"? | Everyone was needed for work at the start of the world | The world was too new to have any deserts in it yet | The Djinn had only just been made that very morning | The animals had not yet learned how to speak
test: Granny in the Black Country: "That Camel day do a thing all week, bab." ("day" = didn't, "bab" = dear.) On which day did the work begin? | Monday | Tuesday | Sunday | Thursday
test: Leo says the moral of the poem is: "If you're bored and grumpy, sit still by the fire with a book." Put him right. | The poem says go and dig outside till you warm up | The poem says go to the zoo and visit the Camel | The poem says the hump goes away if you sleep | The poem says that only kiddies ever get the hump
test: Compare the Camel with the Mariner in the first story. Which is true? | The Mariner was clever and busy; the Camel was idle and rude | Both were lazy and refused to do anything they were asked | The Camel was cleverer, for he tricked a magic Djinn | Both ended up swallowed by a big hungry animal
test: In both stories so far, something changes an animal's body for ever. Which pair is right? | A grating in the Whale's throat; a hump on the Camel's back | A trunk on the Whale's nose; spots on the Camel's back | Folds in the Whale's skin; stripes on the Camel's legs | A pouch on the Whale's tummy; a horn on the Camel's nose
test: Callum, Glasgow: "That 'Stute Fish wis aye feart o' the Whale efter." ("aye" = always, "feart" = afraid.) Where did he hide? | In the mud under the Door-sills of the Equator | Behind the Camel's hump in the Howling Desert | Inside the Mariner's boot on the shingle beach | In a pool of water where the Camel drank
test: Uncle Dave, rough: "Shut up and get on with it, or you'll get the hump like that bloody camel." What is he really telling the kid? | Stop moaning and get busy | You will grow a hump on your back | Go to the desert with the camel | Camels are rude, so stay away
test: ★★★ The Camel stopped working on Monday and got his hump on Thursday. The Djinn says he "missed those three days". Which three days, counting from the story? | Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday | Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday | Monday, Wednesday and Friday | Thursday, Friday and Saturday
test: ★★★ Count the animals that asked the Camel to work. Then add the Man, who never went to the Camel himself. How many came to the Camel in person before the Djinn? | Three | Four | Five | Two
test: ★★★ Kipling's real joke: what is the double meaning of "the hump"? | A lump on a camel's back, and a grumpy mood | A sand dune in the desert, and a magic spell | A heavy load to carry, and a kind of dance | A big meal for later, and a word for a meeting
exam: Identify the three domestic animals which approached the Camel with a request that he undertake labour. | The Horse, the Dog and the Ox | The Horse, the Djinn and the Ox | The Dog, the Whale and the Horse | The Ox, the Man and the Dog
exam: Ascertain the consequence for the remaining animals of the Camel's persistent refusal to labour. | They were obliged to work double-time | They were released from all their labour | They were banished to the Howling Desert | They were each given a hump of their own
exam: Select the response that most accurately states the function of the hump as explained by the Djinn of All Deserts. | It permits labour for three days without food | It permits the Camel to swim across the sea | It serves to carry the Man across the sand | It serves as a punishment devoid of all use
exam: State, in accordance with established biology, the principal substance stored within the hump of a camel. | Fat, upon which the animal may draw when food is scarce | Water, which is released whenever the animal is thirsty | Air, which serves to keep the animal cool in the desert | Sand, which the animal swallows to aid its digestion
